// sb-forgery: expect runtime-panic
// sb-forgery-entry: ReadForgedTenant
//
// Attempt #9: forge a TenantAccess with an empty literal at a brand
// the attacker declares.
//
// Attempt #6 stops at the compiler: `shenguard.TenantAccess{}` names
// no brand, so it is "cannot use generic type … without
// instantiation". That is a real defence, but it is only the first
// half of W1, and attempt #6 cannot demonstrate the second half —
// a file that does not compile never reaches a runtime.
//
// This attempt supplies the missing brand. Declaring `forgedBrand` is
// legal from any package, and `shenguard.TenantAccess[forgedBrand]{}`
// is then a legal expression: unexported fields stop you naming a
// field, they never stopped you taking the zero value. So the forged
// proof exists, is well-typed, and would satisfy any handler that asks
// for one.
//
// Outcome: **panics on first read**. The generated struct's first
// field is an unexported `valid witness`, whose zero value is not
// minted, and every accessor opens with `mustBeMinted`. The moment
// anything actually *uses* the forgery — here, `Tenant()` — the
// program dies with
//
//	shenguard: forged TenantAccess value: not produced by NewTenantAccess
//	(zero value, empty literal, or a dropped constructor error)
//
// That panic is the runtime half of W1, and it is a member of the TCB:
// it is a check that runs, not a check the compiler discharged. It is
// also what catches the *other* zero-value forgery, the one nobody
// writes on purpose — a caller that drops a constructor's error and
// keeps the accompanying zero value. See ../../docs/TRUST-MODEL.md.
//
// The pairing with attempt #6 is the point. Together they say: to
// forge a proof you must first name a brand, which is an explicit and
// greppable act, and then the value you get still detonates the first
// time you read it.
package bypass_attempts_demo

import (
	"multi-tenant-api/internal/shenguard"
)

// forgedBrand is a brand the attacker declares. Nothing stops this;
// brands are ordinary types.
type forgedBrand struct{}

// ForgeEmptyTenantAccess builds the forgery. This much succeeds: the
// expression is legal Go and the value is well-typed.
func ForgeEmptyTenantAccess() shenguard.TenantAccess[forgedBrand] {
	return shenguard.TenantAccess[forgedBrand]{}
}

// ReadForgedTenant is the gate's entry point. Reading the forgery is
// where it dies.
func ReadForgedTenant() string {
	return ForgeEmptyTenantAccess().Tenant().Val()
}
